  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThursday night, the upper ridge is forecast to be centered over the \nCarolinas, and extending down through West Texas and Southeast New \nMexico into Chihuahua.  Remnants of T.D. Edouard are forecast to \npersist over the upper Texas coast, too far east to influence \nconditions here in West Texas and Southeast New Mexico.  A large \ntrough is forecast to reside over the PacNW, extending down the west \ncoast and advecting tropical moisture from T.S. Marie into the \nregion on southwest flow aloft.  NAEFS ensembles increase PWATs over \nthe northwest CWA to 2-3 std devs above normal, and this will \ncombine with easterly upslope flow to keep a chance of convection in \nplay over the higher terrain, especially Thursday night through \nFriday night.  Chances diminish going into the weekend, as the ridge \nis forecast to develop back west, ending up over Oklahoma/Arkansas \nby the end of the forecast period.\n\nHowever, despite the ridge building back west, it will be weak, \nw/thicknesses forecast to decrease into next week.  As a result, \nFriday looks to be the warmest day of the extended forecast, w/highs \naveraging 10-12 F above normal.  Temperatures should then \"cool\" \ninto next week, amounting to only 8-10 F above climatology by \nMonday/Tuesday.  The warmth persists, but the strengthening west \ncoast trough hints at finally breaking down the ridge over the next \nfew weeks.",
